<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">2004 Indian Ocean earthquake and tsunami</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as Sumatra–Andaman earthquake, Boxing Day tsunami, Asian tsunami</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> earthquake and tsunami began on 26 December 2004, when an undersea megathrust earthquake of magnitude 9.2–9.3 struck off the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, along the boundary between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> plate and the Indian plate. It had the longest fault rupture ever observed, 1,200 to 1,300 kilometres, and caused a tsunami with waves up to 30 m high. The tsunami killed an estimated 227,898 people in 14 countries, especially in Aceh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and Khao <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, the deadliest tsunami in history. Donations to the humanitarian response totalled more than US$14 billion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/2004-indian-ocean-earthquake-and-tsunami.jpg" alt="2004 Indian Ocean earthquake and tsunami" width="330" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Navy photo by Photographer's Mate 2nd Class Philip A. McDaniel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Navy_050102-N-9593M-040_A_village_near_the_coast_of_Sumatra_lays_in_ruin_after_the_Tsunami_that_struck_South_East_Asia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
